{"name":"Space Explorer","hex":"#114499","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"space","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Unlimited or generalized extent, physical or otherwise.\nThe distance between objects. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","The distance between objects. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}"]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To roam, walk, wander.","To set some distance apart."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/space"},{"term":"explorer","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["One who explores something","A person who by means of travel (notably an expedition) searches out new information."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/explorer"}],"entries":[{"title":"Space","blocks":[{"text":"Space is a three-dimensional continuum containing positions and directions. In classical physics, physical space is often conceived in three linear dimensions. Modern physicists usually consider it, with time, to be part of a boundless four-dimensional continuum known as spacetime. The concept of space is considered to be of fundamental importance to an understanding of the physical universe. However, disagreement continues between philosophers over whether it is itself an entity, a relationship between entities, or part of a conceptual framework."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/3D_coordinate_system.svg/330px-3D_coordinate_system.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Space","wikidata":"Q107"},{"title":"Exploration","blocks":[{"text":"Exploration is the process of exploring, an activity which has some expectation of discovery. Organised exploration is largely a human activity, but exploratory activity is common to most organisms capable of directed locomotion and the ability to learn, and has been described in, amongst others, social insects foraging behaviour, where feedback from returning individuals affects the activity of other members of the group."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Exploration","wikidata":"Q116967296"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ce/Gottfried_Wilhelm_Leibniz%2C_Bernhard_Christoph_Francke.jpg/500px-Gottfried_Wilhelm_Leibniz%2C_Bernhard_Christoph_Francke.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ce/Gottfried_Wilhelm_Leibniz%2C_Bernhard_Christoph_Francke.jpg/1280px-Gottfried_Wilhelm_Leibniz%2C_Bernhard_Christoph_Francke.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ce/Gottfried_Wilhelm_Leibniz%2C_Bernhard_Christoph_Francke.jpg/500px-Gottfried_Wilhelm_Leibniz%2C_Bernhard_Christoph_Francke.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Gottfried Leibniz","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGottfried_Wilhelm_Leibniz%2C_Bernhard_Christoph_Francke.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/GodfreyKneller-IsaacNewton-1689.jpg/500px-GodfreyKneller-IsaacNewton-1689.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/GodfreyKneller-IsaacNewton-1689.jpg/1280px-GodfreyKneller-IsaacNewton-1689.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/GodfreyKneller-IsaacNewton-1689.jpg/500px-GodfreyKneller-IsaacNewton-1689.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Isaac Newton","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGodfreyKneller-IsaacNewton-1689.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a2/Immanuel_Kant_portrait_c1790.jpg/500px-Immanuel_Kant_portrait_c1790.jpg?utm_source=en.wikiped